"The postoperative pathology demonstrates a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) diagnosis with pathological stage pT2. Reassuring findings include negative surgical margins, no invasion of the seminal vesicles, and absence of lymphovascular invasion. The biopsy identified Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1) in the right peripheral zone. The absence of documented nodal sampling means that the pNx status does not establish node-negative disease, which widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The documented surgical findings are favorable, as noted by the report stating, \"There was no extraprostatic extension; surgical margins were negative; the seminal vesicles were not invaded.\" Conversely, the lack of nodal status information is a significant area of uncertainty. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, meaning biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 81.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 53.15–110.38 months; its coverage has not been validated."
